WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.120
A trading app has millions of users and thousands of trades a second, each one moving a balance.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.239
A user disputes a trade.

00:00:08.239 --> 00:00:11.358
The app showed five thousand dollars when they hit Sell, then it filled lower.

00:00:11.358 --> 00:00:14.478
To settle it, you have to show the exact state of that account at that instant.

00:00:14.478 --> 00:00:17.597
Not now.

00:00:17.597 --> 00:00:20.717
Then.

00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:23.837
And it is not only one upset user.

00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:26.956
Regulators require this.

00:00:26.956 --> 00:00:30.075
The SEC can order a firm to reconstruct any trade, to prove there was no insider trading.

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:33.195
Keeping the history is the law, and it is what earns a user's trust.

00:00:33.195 --> 00:00:36.315
So, could you just copy the whole database, and keep every version?

00:00:36.315 --> 00:00:39.434
No.

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:42.553
Not with thousands of accounts changing it every instant.

00:00:42.553 --> 00:00:45.673
That is billions of rows per second.

00:00:45.673 --> 00:00:48.792
Impossible.

00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:51.912
Same shape everywhere.

00:00:51.912 --> 00:00:55.031
A hospital has to show what a chart said the moment a drug was given.

00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:58.151
An exchange has to replay its order book to the microsecond a crash hit.

00:00:58.151 --> 00:01:01.270
Everyone needs a past version.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.390
Nobody can copy the whole thing to keep one.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.509
That is the version problem.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:10.629
So stop copying and start logging.

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:13.748
Save each change, not the whole database.

00:01:13.748 --> 00:01:16.868
Then replay the changes to reach any version you want.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.465
Two systems you already use do the same two things.

00:01:19.465 --> 00:01:22.062
Start with Google Docs.

00:01:22.062 --> 00:01:24.659
Don't copy.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.256
It never keeps fifty full copies of your document.

00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:29.853
It logs each edit.

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:32.450
Type a sentence.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.048
Delete a paragraph.

00:01:35.048 --> 00:01:37.645
Fix a typo.

00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:40.242
Now, Docs keeps the current document and snapshots itself now and then.

00:01:40.242 --> 00:01:42.839
So to show it an hour ago, it does not rebuild from your first keystroke.

00:01:42.839 --> 00:01:45.436
It starts from a recent snapshot and replays the edits forward.

00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:48.033
That replay is exactly what a database calls redo.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:50.630
Stepping back from where you are is undo.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.661
Git does the same.

00:01:53.661 --> 00:01:56.692
Don't copy.

00:01:56.692 --> 00:01:59.723
A commit is a snapshot, but files that did not change are shared, not recopied.

00:01:59.723 --> 00:02:02.754
The first commit records what changed.

00:02:02.754 --> 00:02:05.785
The second, more changes.

00:02:05.785 --> 00:02:08.816
The third, more still.

00:02:08.816 --> 00:02:11.847
History stays small, with no full copies.

00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:14.878
Any version.

00:02:14.878 --> 00:02:17.909
Check out any commit to see what the code was at that point, or revert to it.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.940
A database does both, at the finest grain.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.971
It logs the old and the new value of every change.

00:02:23.971 --> 00:02:27.002
From that log it rebuilds any past version, which powers an audit, an undo, and, after a crash, recovery.

00:02:27.002 --> 00:02:30.033
Crashes are rare, but the log earns its keep every day.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:33.064
Recovery is next.

00:02:33.064 --> 00:02:36.095
Redo and undo.
